    <text xml:lang="ta" type="edition">
      <body>
        <div rend="parallel">
            <p xml:id="Aink441-kilavi">ceṉṟa viṉai muṭiyāmaiyiṉ kārkālam vantaviṭattu mīḷappeṟāta talaimakaṉ talaimakaḷ uḻainiṉṟum vanta tūtar vārttai kēṭṭu iraṅkiyatu.</p>
            <p xml:lang="en">Because the work for which [he] went is incomplete, the hero who had been unable to return when the rainy season came, having heard the words of the messengers who came from the heroine, wept.</p>
              </div>
        <div rend="parallel">
          <lg xml:id="Aink441">        
            <l>aiya vāyiṉa ceyyōḷ kiḷavi</l>
            <l>kārnā ḷurumoṭu kaiyaṟap pirinteṉa</l>
            <l>nōynaṉku ceytaṉa vemakkē</l>
            <l>yāmuṟu tuyaramava ḷaṟiyiṉō naṉṟē.</l>
        </lg>  
 <lg xml:lang="en">                    
<l>Wondrous<note xml:lang="en" place="foot">The nuance of <foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">aiya</foreign> is unclear to me. As I currently understand it, the heroine's words are wonderous in the sense that they are strongly affecting, perhaps through the strength of their wording or their reflection of her prefection. There does seem to be another way of understand <foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">aiya</foreign>, though, which is to take <foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">aiya</foreign> as <foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">meṉmai</foreign> ('softness'), as suggested by Turaicāmi Piḷḷai, in which case I think the implication is that the words reflect her weakness-in-separation.</note> were<note place="foot" xml:lang="en">The present translation reads <foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">āyiṉa</foreign> ('things that were'), but one could also read <foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">vāyiṉa</foreign> ('things from the mouth', i.e. the heroine's words). In that case the relationship between <foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">aiya vāyiṉa</foreign> and the heroine's words would be attributive rather than predicative. In practice, this would make little difference to the overall sense of the poem.</note> the words of the perfect one.</l>
<l>Along with the thunder of the monsoon days, since [I] separated</l>
<l>so that [she] became desolate, they have caused me great pain.</l>
<l>If she knew the grief that I suffer, it would be good.</l> 
</lg>                      
        </div>
      </body>
    </text>
